### Jet mass scale and resolution

After applying the jet selections mentioned in Sec. 4.4, we can estimate the detector performance using PYTHIA-6 and PYTHIA-6+GEANT events embedded into real zero-bias data from 200 GeV pp collisions in 2012 (cf. Sec. 4.5). For each event, we match particle-level jets to detector-level jets geometrically, requiring the jet axes to be separated by not more than the jet radius, and taking the highest-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) match when more than one jet satisfies this requirement. Fig. 5.3 (5.4) shows the ratio of (groomed) jet mass at detector-level to jet mass at particle-level for various \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) selections. See Appendix D for the figures for \(R=0.2\) and \(R=0.6\). There is a bimodality to the distribution for \(15<p_{\mathrm{T}}<20\) GeV/c that is not evident in the higher-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) selections where the shape is more Gaussian. Similar behavior is seen for the groomed jet mass, although with more populated tails. From these distributions, we can extract the jet mass scale (JMS) and jet mass resolution (JMR), or the mean and width of these distributions, respectively. These are shown in Fig. 5.5. We see that in addition to the fairly large shift of the \(\mathrm{JM_{(g)}S}\) from zero, there is a jet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) dependence, which follows the \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) dependence of the jet momentum scale caused by tracks lost due to tracking efficiency, a greater percentage of which are lost as \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) (and by extension the average number of low-momentum constituent tracks) increases. This \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\)-dependence of the JMS, along with the \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) dependence of the mass itself - see, e.g. Fig. 5.22, motivates the use of a two-dimensional response matrix for the Bayesian unfolding procedure (Sec. 5.3).

The detector performance so far has been shown as a function of jet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\). However, for some subset of jet masses, we may expect a much different performance. Very-low-mass jets, for example, should also have fewer constituents, with higher \(p_{\mathrm{T,cons}}\) on average. Due to momentum-dependent single tracking efficiency and momentum resolution, these jets have potentially been affected by the detector in different ways than the overall jet population. It was seen that for these jets, the detector performance worsens significantly (Fig. 5.6), both in terms of JMR (the width of the distribution) and bimodality. The lower peak, causing the bimodality, is partly due to the misassignment of rest mass at detector-level (Sec. 5.1.3).

Figure 5.5: Upper panels: fractional change of the jet mass (a) and groomed mass (b) in the detector, as a function of particle-level jet \(p_{\rm T}\). Lower panels: The mean for each \(p_{\rm T}\) (star marker) with RMS shown as a vertical error bar (typically smaller than the size of the marker). Note: \(\Delta\) is defined as detector-level minus particle-level.

As an exaggerated example, take a jet with \(M_{\rm part}=3.3\,\)GeV/c\({}^{2}\) with three constituents, all protons, which are then each assigned \(m_{\pi^{+}}\). The mass of the measured jet even excluding any detector effects which tend to decrease the mass, would be about \(0.5\,\)GeV/c\({}^{2}\), which would give \(M_{\rm det}/M_{\rm part}\approx 0.15\). The same rest mass misassignment on a jet which is measured with, say, \(M_{\rm det}=4\,\)GeV/c\({}^{2}\) implies an original particle-level jet with \(M_{\rm part}=6.8\,\)GeV/c\({}^{2}\), which would give \(M_{\rm det}/M_{\rm part}\approx 0.6\). This explains the approach to a normal distribution as detector-level jet mass increases. See Appendix B for a more detailed discussion of the low-jet-mass detector performance.

The resulting selection that we make for this analysis, and that of Ch. 6, of \(M_{\rm det}>1\,\)GeV/c\({}^{2}\) is slightly arbitrary (as can be seen in Fig. 5.7a, which demonstrates the gradual improvement of the jet mass resolution). However, raising the threshold to, say, \(1.5\,\)GeV/c\({}^{2}\) does not dramatically improve the _overall_ jet mass resolution or scale (Fig. 5.7b), and does reduce statistics by roughly 3%. On the other hand, reducing the threshold to, say, \(0.5\,\)GeV/c\({}^{2}\) increases the number of jets by less than 1%, so \(M=1\,\)GeV/c\({}^{2}\) was chosen as the threshold. It was also determined that a selection on the groomed jet mass would be detrimental, because of the irregular phase space selection that would be caused by having a requirement

