## Appendix A Collider kinematic variables and conventions

PositionThe Cartesian coordinate system for position within the STAR experiment (see Fig. A.1) is aligned as follows; \(+z\) points along the beamline roughly west, \(+y\) points perpendicular to the beamline and toward to the sky, and \(+x\) points along the ground, away from the center of the RHIC ring. \((x,y,z)=(0,0,0)\) is set at the center of the STAR barrel. For the most part, the only important direction of these three for our purposes is the \(z\) direction, because the cylindrical symmetry of the detector allows for the use of cylindrical coordinates. Here, the \(x-y\) plane can be reformulated with a radius, \(r\), and angle, \(\varphi\): \(r=\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}\) and \(\varphi=\text{atan2}(y,x)\)1, while \(z\) is unchanged.

Footnote 1: atan2(y,x) is basically \(\arctan(y/x)\), but well-defined for all \(x\) and \(y\).

MomentumBecause we are considering highly relativistic collisions, we replace classical mechanical three-momentum (space) vectors with the equivalent four-momentum (space-time) vectors, with \((E/c,p_{x},p_{y},p_{z})=(E/c,p_{\text{T}},p_{z})\), where \(p_{\text{T}}\), the transverse momentum, is the cylindrical radius in momentum space. For bodies in motion relative to some frame, Einstein's energy equation for the rest energy, \(E=mc^{2}\), is extended to include the kinetic contribution to the total energy:

\[E^{2}=\mathbf{p}^{2}c^{2}+m^{2}c^{4},\] (A.1)

where \(\mathbf{p}\) is the three-momentum. However, in quantum field theory, intermediate particles which do not appear in the final state can violate this relation. The amount to which it is violated is called the virtuality or off-shellness2 of the particle. A higher virtuality of the initiating parton of a jet will produce a jet with a higher invariant mass, \(M=|\sum_{i\in J}p_{i}^{2}|\), where a script \(p\) refers to the four-momentum.

EnergyAn invariant measure of the energy of a collision of two particles 1 and 2 is \(s=(p_{1}+p_{2})^{2}\). In the center-of-mass (CM) frame, where \(\mathbf{p}_{1}+\mathbf{p}_{2}=0\), this simplifies to \(\sqrt{s}=E_{1}+E_{2}\). This is the \(\sqrt{s}\) referred to in Sec. 3.1, for instance. We can also expand the energies using eq. A.1, for a useful formula for calculating \(\sqrt{s}\) if the energy of a nucleon in each beam is known, and assuming \(m\ll E/c^{2}\): \(E\approx 4E_{1}E_{2}\). When nuclei larger than protons are colliding, we use the term \(\sqrt{s_{\mathrm{NN}}}\) to refer to the CM energy per nucleon (N) pair. Energies at RHIC and the LHC are typically given in GeV or TeV, which are giga- (\(10^{9}\)) and tera- (\(10^{12}\)) electron-volts, respectively. An electron-volt is the amount of kinetic energy gained by an electron accelerated from rest by a \(1\,\mathrm{V}\) potential, and its relation to everyday quantities is \(1\,\mathrm{eV}\approx 1.6\times 10^{-19}\,\mathrm{J}\). Using a convention of particle physics called natural units, we set \(c=1=\hbar\), which allows for simple conversion between energy and distance using the relation \(1=\hbar c\approx 197\,\mathrm{MeV}\cdot\mathrm{fm}\) so \(1\,\mathrm{fm}=10^{-15}\,\mathrm{m}\approx 5\,\mathrm{GeV}^{-1}\). Similarly, we have a relation between distance and time: \(1\,\mathrm{fm}\approx 3\times 10^{-24}\,\mathrm{s}\). However, throughout this thesis, natural units are avoided for the purpose of clarity.

Figure A.1: A schematic of the RHIC collider as viewed from above. [143]

RapiditySo far, the angle \(\theta\) in the \(y-z\) plane has not been discussed. This is because it is actually replaced in collider physics by a quantity called rapidity, typically denoted \(y\), but to avoid confusion, we will call it \(\tilde{y}\) in this Appendix. The definition is

\[\tilde{y}=\frac{1}{2}\ln\frac{E+p_{z}c}{E-p_{z}c}.\] (A.2)

The benefit of rapidity, from an experimentalist's point of view, is that relative positions of particles in rapidity space are unaffected by the frame in which they are measured. So as an example, when FastJet calculates the distance between two measured pseudojets, \(d_{ij}\), using \(\Delta R_{ij}^{2}=(\Delta\tilde{y})^{2}+(\Delta\phi)^{2}\) (eq. 2.2), the fact that the measurement was made in the frame of the observer rather than, say, the CM (zero momentum) frame of the collision, is immaterial. These can be different even for symmetric collisions, due to e.g. varying longitudinal momentum fractions (Sec. 1.3) carried by the partons which interact. In asymmetric collisions, this is even more important, due to the large Lorentz boost in the longitudinal direction between the observation frame and the CM frame of the collision (e.g. 5.02 TeV pPb collisions at the LHC, which have \(\Delta\tilde{y}=0.465\) (eq. 2.7 of Ref. [78]) in the proton-going direction3 compared to the observation frame). This is caused by the different energy-per-nucleon of each beam (see Sec. 3.1 for an explanation of this difference in energy).

Footnote 3: In pAu collisions at RHIC, we take the proton-going direction to be positive \(\tilde{y}\) (and \(z\)).

Lastly, it should be noted that even rapidity is often supplanted by a more accessible quantity called "pseudorapidity", \(\eta\), defined as

\[\eta=\frac{1}{2}\ln\frac{|\mathbf{p}|+p_{z}}{|\mathbf{p}|-p_{z}}.\] (A.3)

This definition is useful because the energy of particles is not always known - for instance, in the TPC (Ref. 3.2.1), we measure momentum rather than energy. For massless particles, the two definitions are equivalent. For massive particles in the high-energy limit, using eq. A.1 and letting \(E\gg mc^{2}\), we have \(E^{2}\approx\mathbf{p}^{2}c^{2}\), so \(|\tilde{y}|-|\eta|\to 0^{-}\) as \(E\to\infty\). Note that due to this agreement at high energy, often the word "rapidity" is used, when what is meant is "pseudorapidity" (including multiple instances in the chapters of this thesis).

