\(5\,\mathrm{GeV/c}\) intervals, followed by larger bins, \((50,60)\) and \((60,80)\,\mathrm{GeV/c}\), where statistics are less important).

After these 1 million events per \(\hat{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\) are generated, we must stitch them together to form a physical steeply-falling jet spectrum, e.g. like the one in Fig. 1.5. This is done by summing the values given by

pythia.info.sigmaGen(0) for each event and dividing by

pythia.info.weightSum() after all events have been recorded, with a similar procedure for HERWIG. This gives the average cross section for all allowed processes in this \(\hat{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\)-bin. Then, when jets are clustered and filled into trees and histograms, the jet observables are weighted by this cross section weight depending on which \(\hat{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\)-bin they originated from. For the most part, this gives a smooth, steeply-falling jet spectrum. However, since the partonic cross section does not exactly set the jet cross section, it is possible in rare cases to obtain jets with \(p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}\gg\hat{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\). Since the cross section weight diminishes monotonically as \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) increases, these few jets will receive a large weight, compared to jets of the same \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) from a more typical \(\hat{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\) bin. The spectrum will then have unphysical spikes with large uncertainties. To reduce this effect, in the analysis we veto events with a jet of \(p_{\mathrm{T}}>2\hat{p}_{\mathrm{T}}-\)max. This gives a reasonable cross section.

